-- title:
--   Integrality of q-expansions of two-cusp integral weight-two forms
-- statement:
--   Let $p$ be a prime, let $M \ge 1$, let $H$ be a subgroup of $(\mathbb{Z}/M)^\times$, let $W$ be an Atkin–Lehner datum for $(M,p)$ — that is, a factorisation $M = pR$ together with integers $a,b$ satisfying $pa - Rb = 1$ — and let $e \in (\mathbb{Z}/M)^\times$. Let $f$ be a weight-two cusp form for the congruence subgroup $\Gamma_H(M) \le \mathrm{SL}(2,\mathbb{Z})$, the preimage of $H$ under the map $\Gamma_0(M) \to (\mathbb{Z}/M)^\times$ sending $\gamma$ to its lower-right entry modulo $M$, viewed inside $\mathrm{GL}(2,\mathbb{R})$. Assume $f$ lies in the two-cusp integral set for the subring $\bot \subseteq \mathbb{C}$, i.e. for every element $t$ of the Hecke ring `heckeRingH` in weight $2$ (the subring of $\mathbb{C}$-endomorphisms of $S_2(\Gamma_H(M))$ generated by the operators `heckeGenH`), every Atkin–Lehner datum $W'$ for $(M,p)$ and every $n \in \mathbb{N}$, the $n$-th coefficients of the $q$-expansions (of width $1$) of $tf$ and of $(tf)\mid_2 W'$ are rational integers. The conclusion is twofold: there is a power series $p_f \in \mathbb{Z}[\![q]\!]$ whose image in $\mathbb{C}[\![q]\!]$ is the $q$-expansion of $f$; and there is $p_{fW} \in \mathbb{Z}[\![q]\!]$ whose image in $\mathbb{C}[\![q]\!]$ is the $q$-expansion of $(\langle e\rangle f)\mid_2 W$, where $\langle e\rangle$ is `diamondLinH` (slashing by a lift of $e$ when `StableD` holds in weight $2$, and $0$ otherwise) and $\mid_2 W$ is slashing in weight $2$ by the real matrix attached to $W$.
--
--   This is the statement that a weight-two form integral at the two relevant cusps genuinely has integral Fourier coefficients there, in the form of honest power series over $\mathbb{Z}$ rather than membership of coefficients in a subring. It feeds the two-cusp $q$-expansion principle and the construction of the reduction maps into regular differentials, being cited by [`ModularCurve.exists_linearEquiv_intTwoCuspForms_twoCompRegularDifferentials`](thm.html#ModularCurve.exists_linearEquiv_intTwoCuspForms_twoCompRegularDifferentials) and the lemmas on `twoCompRegularDifferentials` and `ssPolarDifferentials`.
-- source:
